{
  "dom_id": "step-q214316",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268926/assets/2cab03969d444f76a99c1f9812dc015cf4e1adc668b77fc531b79b3b0f502809.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a vacuously transitive relation. There are no pairs of vertices for which $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z.$\n\n- Digraph II does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $3\\to 1$ and $1\\to 2,$ but has no arrow $3\\to 2.$\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $2\\to 3$ and $3\\to 2,$ but has no arrow $2\\to 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
